    "final_message": "## Summary\n\nI've successfully completed the red acceptance test for the story-drafting user story:\n\n**Test file:** `<repo>/story-drafting/tests/test_acceptance.py`\n\n**Test name:** `TestSubmitAndCheckStatus.test_submitted_jtbd_status_is_drafting`\n\n**Test behavior:**\n1. Submits a JTBD via HTTP POST with `status=\"waiting_for_pick\"`\n2. Immediately reads the JTBD back via HTTP GET\n3. Asserts that the HTTP status codes are 201 (created) and 200 (ok)\n4. **Fails** at the assertion that `get_status[\"status\"] == \"drafting\"`\n\n**Failure message:**\n```\nAssertionError: assert 'waiting_for_pick' == 'drafting'\n```\n\nThe test is a proper red test because:\n- \u2705 It's a root test (drives the app only through the HTTP host's protocol)\n- \u2705 The code passes tessercheck with zero findings\n- \u2705 The code passes `mypy --strict`\n- \u2705 The submit and read operations both succeed\n- \u2705 The test fails at the user story's \"then\" (expecting status to be \"drafting\")\n- \u2705 The tree has the complete structure (client, service, workflow, handler, repository) but not the behavior (the status is not set to \"drafting\" by the workflow)\n\nThe spec models the user story's domain with a `Jtbd` aggregate that has a `status` field, tracked through a Restate workflow via HTTP endpoints at `/jtbds` for POST (submit) and `/jtbds/{jtbd_id}` for GET (check status)."
